1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,337
No alarm clock. No inbox.

2
00:00:03,337 --> 00:00:07,500
No commute. So what filled a day before farms and cities?

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:10,613
Picture a small camp waking up near a river.

4
00:00:10,613 --> 00:00:13,787
There is food to find, work to share, and news to catch

5
00:00:13,787 --> 00:00:18,325
up on. Welcome to a possible day in the world of hunter-gatherers.

6
00:00:19,625 --> 00:00:21,712
First, a reality check.

7
00:00:21,712 --> 00:00:24,712
There was no single prehistoric lifestyle.

8
00:00:24,712 --> 00:00:28,025
People lived in different climates, ate different foods,

9
00:00:28,025 --> 00:00:31,038
and changed their routines with the seasons.

10
00:00:31,038 --> 00:00:33,450
Archaeology gives us clues.

11
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:37,825
Studies of more recent foraging societies offer comparisons,

12
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:41,125
not a perfect window into the distant past.

13
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,475
The morning begins with decisions.

14
00:00:44,475 --> 00:00:46,125
Where did the animals move?

15
00:00:46,125 --> 00:00:47,575
Which plants are ready?

16
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:49,713
Is there water along the route?

17
00:00:49,713 --> 00:00:52,900
A footprint, a broken branch, or a change in the weather

18
00:00:52,900 --> 00:00:54,625
can shape the plan.

19
00:00:54,625 --> 00:00:58,225
Knowing the landscape is a skill learned over years, not

20
00:00:58,225 --> 00:00:59,700
a lucky guess.

21
00:01:00,825 --> 00:01:03,813
Finding food is more than a dramatic hunt.

22
00:01:03,813 --> 00:01:07,025
Depending on the place and season, meals might include

23
00:01:07,025 --> 00:01:11,375
roots, nuts, fruit, fish, shellfish, and meat.

24
00:01:11,375 --> 00:01:15,900
Tasks can overlap, and who does what varies between communities.

25
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:19,175
Several people collecting reliable foods may matter

26
00:01:19,175 --> 00:01:21,725
as much as one successful hunter.

27
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,700
And hunting? It takes patience, coordination, and a tolerance

28
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,400
for failure. The animal does not follow your schedule.

29
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:34,025
A trip may bring back meat, or nothing at all.

30
00:01:34,025 --> 00:01:36,900
Sharing food within a group can help people cope with

31
00:01:36,900 --> 00:01:38,400
that uncertainty.

32
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:41,000
Survival is a social project.

33
00:01:42,075 --> 00:01:44,463
Back at camp, the work is not finished.

34
00:01:44,463 --> 00:01:48,075
Food may need cleaning, cutting, pounding, or cooking.

35
00:01:48,075 --> 00:01:50,075
Someone repairs a handle.

36
00:01:50,075 --> 00:01:52,575
Someone sharpens a stone edge.

37
00:01:52,575 --> 00:01:54,388
Someone tends the fire.

38
00:01:54,388 --> 00:01:57,350
The tools look simple in a museum case.

39
00:01:57,350 --> 00:02:00,300
Making and maintaining them takes practical knowledge

40
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:01,975
and careful hands.

41
00:02:03,075 --> 00:02:06,463
Children learn through watching, playing, and trying small

42
00:02:06,463 --> 00:02:10,625
tasks. A game can become practice in balance, tracking,

43
00:02:10,625 --> 00:02:12,325
or making things.

44
00:02:12,325 --> 00:02:15,375
Adults and other children pass along knowledge through

45
00:02:15,375 --> 00:02:18,863
everyday life. Learning does not need a classroom, but

46
00:02:18,863 --> 00:02:22,800
it still takes teachers, attention, and plenty of mistakes.

47
00:02:24,025 --> 00:02:26,625
Between tasks, there is conversation.

48
00:02:26,625 --> 00:02:28,950
Who needs help? Who found food?

49
00:02:28,950 --> 00:02:30,638
Who is annoyed with whom?

50
00:02:30,638 --> 00:02:33,925
Relationships matter when people depend on one another.

51
00:02:33,925 --> 00:02:37,300
Older people can contribute experience, stories, and

52
00:02:37,300 --> 00:02:41,125
care. A camp is not just a collection of workers.

53
00:02:41,125 --> 00:02:43,625
It is a network of relationships.

54
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:46,938
There can be time to rest, too.

55
00:02:46,938 --> 00:02:49,913
But claims that all hunter-gatherers worked only a few

56
00:02:49,913 --> 00:02:52,963
hours a day hide a complicated picture.

57
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:56,638
The answer depends on the community, the season, and what

58
00:02:56,638 --> 00:03:01,125
counts as work. Food preparation, childcare, and toolmaking

59
00:03:01,125 --> 00:03:04,625
do not disappear just because nobody clocks in.

60
00:03:05,725 --> 00:03:07,988
This is not a lost paradise.

61
00:03:07,988 --> 00:03:12,338
An injury, an infection, a dry season, or an empty patch

62
00:03:12,338 --> 00:03:14,763
of land can make life dangerous.

63
00:03:14,763 --> 00:03:18,263
Cooperation helps, but it does not erase risk.

64
00:03:18,263 --> 00:03:21,613
The same landscape that provides dinner can force difficult

65
00:03:21,613 --> 00:03:25,675
choices about moving, sharing, and what to try next.

66
00:03:26,775 --> 00:03:29,588
As daylight fades, the pace changes.

67
00:03:29,588 --> 00:03:32,775
Around a fire, there may be food to share and stories

68
00:03:32,775 --> 00:03:36,900
to tell. Those stories can entertain, carry knowledge,

69
00:03:36,900 --> 00:03:40,163
or explain how people understand their world.

70
00:03:40,163 --> 00:03:43,563
We cannot recover every ancient conversation.

71
00:03:43,563 --> 00:03:47,225
We can recognize that human life has always involved more

72
00:03:47,225 --> 00:03:48,700
than calories.

73
00:03:49,825 --> 00:03:52,325
So what did ancient humans do all day?

74
00:03:52,325 --> 00:03:55,875
They found food, made things, raised children, managed

75
00:03:55,875 --> 00:03:58,138
relationships, and adapted.

76
00:03:58,138 --> 00:04:01,313
The balance changed across places and times.

77
00:04:01,313 --> 00:04:04,613
Their world was different from ours, but some priorities

78
00:04:04,613 --> 00:04:08,825
feel familiar: a meal, a useful skill, people you trust,

79
00:04:08,825 --> 00:04:10,850
and a little time to breathe.
